  <!-- Panel-left / panel-right (Lucide): rounded-rect (x3 y3 18×18 rx2) + a vertical
       divider at x9 / x15. Drive the sidebar collapse + Saved-Queries-panel toggles. -->
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="Icon.PanelLeft">M5 3 H19 A2 2 0 0 1 21 5 V19 A2 2 0 0 1 19 21 H5 A2 2 0 0 1 3 19 V5 A2 2 0 0 1 5 3 Z M9 3 V21</StreamGeometry>
  <!-- Hamburger — opens the Application Menu, and the FIRST button of the titlebar's action zone
       (docs/design/hamburger-navigation.md §6).

       ⚠ DELIBERATELY NOT verbatim Lucide `menu` (x4→20 at y6/12/18), for two measured reasons.

       (1) SIZE. This ControlTheme wraps a FIXED 24×24 Canvas in the Viewbox, so the Viewbox scales the CANVAS,
       never the path's ink — every icon renders at the same 24→16 scale, and an icon's optical size is purely
       a function of how much of the 24×24 box its geometry fills. Ink box = path extremes ±1 (half of the 2px
       stroke). Neighbours: PanelLeft 20×20 · Trash 20×22 · Copy 22×22 · FolderPlus 22×19 · Plus 16×16.
       Lucide's menu is 18×14 — the shortest glyph on the bar, and it showed.

       (2) ⭐ SUB-PIXEL PHASE — why the LINE SPACING IS 9 AND MUST STAY A MULTIPLE OF 3.
       The 24→16 scale is ×2/3, so a rule declared at y renders its top edge at 2(y−1)/3 with a 1.333px
       thickness. The fractional part of that edge decides the anti-aliasing, so two rules at different
       fractions are DRAWN DIFFERENTLY however symmetric the coordinates look:

         y=4  → band [2.000, 3.333] → row 2 at 100%, row 3 at 33%  → crisp, faint edge below
         y=12 → band [7.333, 8.667] → rows 7 and 8 at 67% EACH     → two grey rows: softer, reads THICKER
         y=20 → band [12.667, 14.000] → row 12 at 33%, row 13 100% → crisp, faint edge above; its round cap
                                                                     lands on another phase, so the right end
                                                                     looks clipped next to the others

       That was one cause with two symptoms. Identical rendering requires equal phases, i.e. 2·Δy/3 ∈ ℤ, so
       **Δy must be a multiple of 1.5** (1.5 · 2/3 = 1). Δy = 7.5 puts all three top edges on phase .333.

       (3) ⚠⚠ AND THE SIZE TARGET IS OPTICAL, NOT GEOMETRIC — the lesson that cost a third QA round.
       Matching PanelLeft's ink box exactly (Δy=9 → 20×20) made the hamburger look BIGGER than its neighbours
       and dominate the bar as its first item. Equal boxes are not equal weight: three full-width rules are far
       denser than a thin rectangle OUTLINE, so at the same extent the hamburger must read heavier. A
       high-density glyph therefore needs a SMALLER box to look the same size, and "ink box == neighbour" was a
       measurable proxy standing in for the real goal.

       Shipped: x4→20 at y4.5/12/19.5 → ink 18×17 against PanelLeft's 20×20 — deliberately smaller in both
       axes, chosen by eye from a side-by-side sheet of candidates rather than by arithmetic. Symmetric by
       construction (both spans centred on 12) and phase-consistent per (2). Tighter/looser one-line steps, all
       phase-safe: y6/12/18 → 18×14 (verbatim Lucide, reads small) · x3→21 → 20×17 · x4.5→19.5 → 17×17.

       ⚠ Pixel-exactness holds at the rendered 16px — the ControlTheme default, and the only size this glyph
       uses. A host that overrode Width/Height would re-scale the 24-unit grid and change the phase; that is
       inherent to scaling, not fixable by coordinates. -->
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="Icon.Menu">M4 4.5 H20 M4 12 H20 M4 19.5 H20</StreamGeometry>
